+SDG is, however, hardest when data are small. Oversampling techniques, such as the Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique (SMOTE)~\cite{chawla2002smote}, and deep generative models, such as the conditional tabular generative adversarial network (CTGAN) and the tabular variational autoencoder (TVAE)~\cite{xu2019ctgan}, learn the distribution from the same few records they are meant to complement, recombining patterns that already exist in those records, for example by interpolating between two existing patients~\cite[Sec.~2.8]{cover2006elements}. Large language models (LLMs) offer a new perspective to address such issue. They are pre-trained on large text corpora, which gives them domain knowledge about healthcare variables, such as plausible ranges and typical relations between them, and they can generate new records from a few real examples placed in the prompt, without any training on the target dataset~\cite{borisov2023great,seedat2024cllm}. Their output, however, is text that has to be parsed into records, and nothing guarantees that it forms a dataset: a response may contain fewer records than requested, malformed records, missing or invented columns, or values of the wrong type (Figure~\ref{fig:teaser}). Every augmentation method is configured with the number of synthetic records to add, usually expressed as a proportion of the real training set~\cite{chawla2002smote}, and a generator that silently returns fewer or malformed records changes the training data without the user noticing. In addition, each request to a commercial LLM has a monetary cost, proportional to the number of tokens (word fragments) processed, which is rarely reported.
