 To answer these questions, an experimental setup was built with six public healthcare datasets containing between 195 and 768 records. For each dataset, synthetic records were produced by \system with seven commercial LLMs from two providers, by direct prompting of the same LLMs, with and without the provider's option to force responses into a fixed format, and by three established generation strategies, SMOTE, CTGAN and TVAE. Each dataset was randomly divided ten times into a training and a test set, and on each division ten classifiers were trained with and without synthetic records and compared on the same test set. The main conclusions are the following. Without the control layer, the LLM never returned the configured number of records, even when its responses were forced into the format of the data, whereas \system completed every dataset at only 1.9\% more cost (RQ1). The synthetic records reproduced the distribution of each real feature with a fidelity of 73 out of 100, and a membership-inference attack could not reliably identify the real records used, although a classifier could still tell synthetic from real records (RQ2). The effect on classifiers depended on the dataset and the classifier, and with the strongest LLMs the average F1-score increased by 1.1 to 2.4 percentage points (RQ3). \system gave the largest and most stable gain among the generators compared: synthetic records can also harm a classifier when they misrepresent the real data, and the established generators caused losses of up to 10.6 points on individual datasets, against at most 0.53 points for \system with GPT-4.1; only the choice of LLM changed the results by more than the variation between repeated runs (RQ4).
 
