 \section{Discussion and Limitations}
 \label{sec:discussion}
 %======================================================================
-The objective of this work was to determine whether \system makes LLM-based SDG a reliable and useful option for small healthcare datasets. Reliability is achieved, and it is achieved by the control layer: the same LLM, prompted directly, never delivered the configured dataset, whereas \system completed it, recorded its cost and caught the failures of a less disciplined model at almost no extra cost (RQ1). Usefulness is real but bounded: the synthetic records are faithful without revealing real ones (RQ2), they help the classifiers that fail on the minority class (RQ3), and with a stronger LLM they give the largest and most stable gain among the generators compared (RQ4). No generator, however, improves every dataset, which is consistent with the data-processing inequality~\cite{cover2006elements}: SDG cannot replace missing patients, and its benefit is bounded by the knowledge the generator brings. Among the generators compared, only the LLM brings such knowledge, and the control layer is what makes it usable. In practice, the choice of LLM matters most, cost is better measured in tokens than in time, and the effect of synthetic records should be checked per classifier before they are used.
+The objective of this work was to determine whether \system makes LLM-based SDG a reliable and useful option for small healthcare datasets. Reliability is achieved, and it is achieved by the control layer: the same LLM, prompted directly, never delivered the configured dataset, whereas \system completed it, recorded its cost and caught the failures of a less disciplined model at almost no extra cost (RQ1). Usefulness is real but bounded: the synthetic records preserve the distribution of the real data without revealing real records (RQ2), they help the classifiers that fail on the minority class (RQ3), and with a stronger LLM they give the largest and most stable gain among the generators compared (RQ4). No generator, however, improves every dataset, which is consistent with the data-processing inequality~\cite[Sec.~2.8]{cover2006elements}: SDG cannot replace missing patients, and its benefit is bounded by the knowledge the generator brings. Among the generators compared, only the LLM brings such knowledge, and the control layer is what makes it usable. In practice, the choice of LLM matters most, cost is better measured in tokens than in time, and the effect of synthetic records should be checked per classifier before they are used.
 
 The conclusions are subject to several limitations. Only six datasets act as independent blocks, which limits the power of the tests, and the divisions are not stratified. The datasets are public and widely used, so they may be part of the LLMs' training data and part of the fidelity may reflect memorized records rather than generation; the membership-inference attack does not test for this, and an evaluation on private data is left for future work. The Parkinson's dataset contains several recordings per subject, so recordings of one person can appear in both sets. The columns and types are inferred from the full dataset rather than from the training set alone. The parameters in Table~\ref{tab:params} were fixed and not tuned, and only two of them were varied, through the LLM-chosen settings variant. \system does not filter synthetic records that are close to real ones: privacy is measured with one distance-based attack, not enforced, and no result establishes differential privacy~\cite{dwork2014dp}. The \system runs predate the counter of delivered records, so their size follows from the completion loop rather than from a logged count, and the validity measure was logged only for the established generators and direct prompting (78.6\% valid records). The corrections were exercised only by GPT-4.1 mini, and records obtained in the last correction round or in the completion loop are not validated again. Context selection is not class-stratified, so a rare class can be missing from a prompt. The LLMs come from two commercial providers and locally served models were not evaluated; one of them, Claude Haiku 5.5, declined to imitate patient records under the control layer's prompts, which shows that a provider's safety behaviour can block generation on healthcare data. CTGAN and TVAE use default settings.
 
